{% assign pf = site.data.policy_feasibility %}
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=IBM+Plex+Sans:wght@400;500;600&display=swap">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ '/assets/css/policy-feasibility.css' | relative_url | bust_file_cache }}">
<div
  id="policy-feasibility"
  class="pf-project"
  data-pf-map="{{ '/assets/data/policy-feasibility/countries.geojson' | relative_url | bust_file_cache }}"
>
  <div class="pf-intro">
    <p class="pf-caption">
      A companion to the research article
      <a href="{{ pf.article_url }}"><em>The politics of baselines</em> · Fornborg (2026)</a>, published in ERSS
    </p>
    <p>
      Energy and climate scenarios depend on assumptions about future policy. This example uses Swedish data centres to show how public discontent,
      actor positions and societal trends shape the political feasibility of possible policy responses.
    </p>
  </div>
  <section id="pf-define" class="pf-section pf-case-intro">
    <div class="pf-case-copy">
      <h2><span class="pf-section-number">01</span> The policy question</h2>
      <p class="pf-question">{{ pf.scope.question }}</p>
      <p class="pf-caption">Sweden · Policy decisions in {{ pf.decision_period }} · Follow to {{ pf.model_horizon }}</p>
    </div>
    <img
      class="pf-case-illustration"
      src="{{ '/assets/images/policy-feasibility/datacentre-linework.png' | relative_url }}"
      alt=""
      width="1714"
      height="918"
      decoding="async"
    >
    <div class="pf-case-context">
      <figure>
        {% include policy-feasibility/tracker-map.liquid %}
        <figcaption>
          {{ site.data.policy_feasibility_context.metric }}.<br>
          Announced operation by {{ site.data.policy_feasibility_context.year }}.<br>
          <a href="{{ site.data.policy_feasibility_context.source_url | relative_url }}"
            >Tracker · {{ site.data.policy_feasibility_context.scenario_label -}}
          </a>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div>
        <h3>Why the rules are contested</h3>
        <p>
          Data centres attract investment and support AI ambitions. Their electricity demand can also raise household bills and delay industrial
          electrification when generation and grid capacity expand slowly.
        </p>
        <details class="pf-citation-note">
          <summary>Sources and map</summary>
          <p class="pf-caption">
            The map shows {{ site.data.policy_feasibility_context.scope | downcase }}. Tracker snapshot:
            {{ site.data.policy_feasibility_context.snapshot_date | date: '%-d %B %Y' }}.
          </p>
          {% assign context_refs = 'E77,E83,E84' | split: ',' %}
          {% include policy-feasibility/references.liquid ids=context_refs %}
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <figure class="pf-method" aria-labelledby="pf-framework-title">
    <figcaption>
      <h2 id="pf-framework-title">The framework</h2>
      <p>
        Drawing on Kahneman and Lovallo (1993), the framework is divided into a scoping outside-view and an assessing inside-view. The outside view is
        sweeping and draws on precedents, comparison and public debate to identify plausible policy trajectories. The inside view compares the
        feasibility of the trajectories along five dimensions informed by political economy and socio-technical theory. Who wins and loses from the
        policy? What actor constellations can support or oppose the policy? What societal trends and institutional contexts are they embedded in?
      </p>
    </figcaption>
    <div class="pf-flow">
      <div class="pf-flow-group">
        <p class="pf-flow-label">Identify plausible trajectories</p>
        <a class="pf-flow-outside" href="#pf-scope"><strong>Outside view</strong><span>Places, time and actors</span></a>
        {% include policy-feasibility/flow-arrow.liquid step='outside' %}
        <a class="pf-flow-alternatives" href="#pf-trajectories"><strong>Policy alternatives</strong><span>Comparable trajectories</span></a>
      </div>
      {% include policy-feasibility/flow-arrow.liquid step='inside' %}
      <a class="pf-flow-inside" href="#pf-assess"><strong>Inside view</strong><span>Five political dimensions</span></a>
      {% include policy-feasibility/flow-arrow.liquid step='scenario' %}
      <a class="pf-flow-scenario" href="#pf-model"><strong>Scenario use</strong><span>Policy assumptions in the model</span></a>
    </div>
  </figure>
  <nav class="pf-dimension-guide" aria-label="The five political dimensions">
    <p class="pf-caption">Five questions guide the investigation and comparison</p>
    <ol>
      {% for d in pf.dimensions %}
        <li>
          <a href="#pf-dimension-{{ d.id }}"
            ><strong>{{ d.name }}</strong><span>{{ d.guide }}</span></a
          >
        </li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ol>
  </nav>
  {% include policy-feasibility/outside.liquid %}
  <section id="pf-trajectories" class="pf-section">
    <h2><span class="pf-section-number">03</span> Define comparable trajectories</h2>
    <p class="pf-prose">{{ pf.derivation }}</p>
    <div class="pf-pathways">
      {% for t in pf.trajectories %}
        <article class="pf-pathway" data-trajectory="{{ t.id }}">
          <p class="pf-caption">{{ t.id }} · {{ t.status }}</p>
          <h3>{{ t.title }}</h3>
          <p class="pf-pathway-summary">{{ t.summary }}</p>
          <p class="pf-origin"><span>From the outside view</span>{{ t.origin_summary }}</p>
          <details>
            <summary>Policy design and sources</summary>
            <p>{{ t.origin }}</p>
            <p>{{ t.decisions }}</p>
            <p>{{ t.example }}</p>
            {% include policy-feasibility/references.liquid ids=t.evidence %}
          </details>
        </article>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
    <details class="pf-note">
      <summary>What the generation requirement covers</summary>
      <p>{{ pf.scope.definition }}</p>
      <p>{{ pf.scope.cohort }}</p>
      <p>{{ pf.scope.timing }}</p>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section id="pf-assess" class="pf-section">
    <h2><span class="pf-section-number">04</span> Compare political feasibility</h2>
    <p class="pf-prose">
      Compare two policies across the five dimensions. The starting assessment uses the evidence available in October 2026 to judge their routes to
      adoption in 2027–2030.
    </p>
    <div class="pf-pair-control" hidden>
      <label for="pf-pair-select">Policy pair</label>
      <select id="pf-pair-select">
        {% for pair in pf.pairs %}
          <option value="{{ pair.id }}">{{ pair.label }}</option>
        {% endfor %}
      </select>
    </div>
    <p id="pf-control-status" class="pf-sr-only" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
    {% for pair in pf.pairs %}
      {% assign first_id = pair.id | split: '-' | first %}
      {% assign second_id = pair.id | split: '-' | last %}
      {% assign first = pf.trajectories | where: 'id', first_id | first %}
      {% assign second = pf.trajectories | where: 'id', second_id | first %}
      <div class="pf-comparison-title" data-pf-comparison="{{ pair.id }}">
        <span
          ><small>{{ first.id }}</small>{{ first.short_title -}}</span
        ><span class="pf-versus">compared with</span
        ><span
          ><small>{{ second.id }}</small>{{ second.short_title -}}
        </span>
      </div>
    {% endfor %}
    <div id="pf-comparisons">
      {% for d in pf.dimensions %}
        <details class="pf-dimension" id="pf-dimension-{{ d.id }}">
          <summary>
            <span class="pf-dimension-name"
              ><strong>{{ d.name }}</strong><span>{{ d.guide }}</span></span
            >
            <span class="pf-pair-verdicts">
              {% for pair in pf.pairs %}
                <span class="pf-pair-verdict" data-pf-comparison="{{ pair.id }}">
                  <span class="pf-pair-name pf-caption">{{ pair.label }}</span>
                  <span class="pf-initial-verdict">{{ d.pairs[pair.id].takeaway }}</span>
                </span>
              {% endfor %}
            </span>
          </summary>
          <div class="pf-dimension-body">
            <p class="pf-caption">{{ d.description }}</p>
            <div class="pf-comparison-columns">
              {% for t in pf.trajectories %}
                <article data-pf-trajectory="{{ t.id }}">
                  <h3>{{ t.short_title }}</h3>
                  <p>{{ d.cells[t.id].assessment }}</p>
                </article>
              {% endfor %}
            </div>
            {% for pairing in pf.pairs %}
              {% assign pair = d.pairs[pairing.id] %}
              {% assign first_id = pairing.id | split: '-' | first %}
              {% assign second_id = pairing.id | split: '-' | last %}
              {% assign refs = pair.evidence
                | concat: d.cells[first_id].evidence
                | concat: d.cells[second_id].evidence
                | concat: d.literature
                | uniq
              %}
              <details class="pf-citation-note" data-pf-comparison="{{ pairing.id }}">
                <summary>Sources and research</summary>
                {% include policy-feasibility/references.liquid ids=refs %}
              </details>
            {% endfor %}
          </div>
        </details>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
    {% for pair in pf.pairs %}
      <div class="pf-starting-conclusion pf-conclusion" data-pf-comparison="{{ pair.id }}">
        <p class="pf-caption">Starting assessment · Evidence to October 2026</p>
        <p>{{ pf.initial_synthesis[pair.id] }}</p>
      </div>
    {% endfor %}
    <p class="pf-caption pf-comparison-reference">
      Pairwise comparison: Fornborg (2026), §4.5, drawing on <a href="{{ pf.pairwise_reference.url }}">Carlson and Montgomery (2017)</a>.
    </p>
    <div id="pf-conditions" class="pf-reassessment">
      <div class="pf-reassessment-intro">
        <img
          src="{{ '/assets/images/policy-feasibility/electricity-users-linework.png' | relative_url }}"
          alt=""
          width="1536"
          height="1024"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        >
        <div>
          <p class="pf-caption">Before the decision · 2027–2029</p>
          <h3>How do the assumptions affect the comparison?</h3>
          <p>Start with constrained generation and grid expansion. Test how greater capacity availability changes the policy comparison.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pf-development-control" hidden>
        <p class="pf-caption">Explore the assumptions</p>
        <div class="pf-assumption-controls">
          {% for input in pf.sensitivity_inputs %}
            <fieldset>
              <legend>{{ input.label }}</legend>
              <div class="pf-development-options">
                {% for option in input.options %}
                  <button
                    type="button"
                    data-pf-assumption="{{ input.id }}"
                    data-pf-value="{{ option.id }}"
                    aria-pressed="{% if forloop.first %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}"
                    aria-controls="pf-reassessment-content pf-model"
                  >
                    <span class="pf-assumption-role">{{ option.role }}</span>{{ option.label }}
                  </button>
                {% endfor %}
              </div>
            </fieldset>
          {% endfor %}
        </div>
      </div>
      <div id="pf-reassessment-content">
        {% for development in pf.developments %}
          <div class="pf-development-reading" data-pf-development="{{ development.id }}">
            <h4 class="pf-fallback-label">{{ development.label }}</h4>
            <p class="pf-condition-description">{{ development.assumption }}</p>
            <dl class="pf-assumption-effects">
              {% for effect in development.effects %}
                <div>
                  <dt>{{ effect.label }}</dt>
                  <dd>{{ effect.text }}</dd>
                </div>
              {% endfor %}
            </dl>
            {% for pair in pf.pairs %}
              <div class="pf-decision-change pf-conclusion" data-pf-comparison="{{ pair.id }}">
                <p class="pf-caption">{{ pair.label }} · Adoption by 2030</p>
                <h4>{{ development.adoption[pair.id].title }}</h4>
                <p>{{ development.adoption[pair.id].text }}</p>
                <details class="pf-note pf-reassessment-dimensions">
                  <summary>Reasoning across the five dimensions</summary>
                  <dl>
                    {% for d in pf.dimensions %}
                      <div>
                        <dt>{{ d.name }}</dt>
                        <dd>{{ d.developments[development.id][pair.id] }}</dd>
                      </div>
                    {% endfor %}
                  </dl>
                </details>
              </div>
            {% endfor %}
            <details class="pf-citation-note">
              <summary>Evidence used in this comparison</summary>
              {% include policy-feasibility/references.liquid ids=development.evidence %}
            </details>
          </div>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="pf-model" class="pf-section">
    <h2><span class="pf-section-number">05</span> Bring the assessment into the scenario</h2>
    <div class="pf-assumption-change">
      <div>
        <p class="pf-caption">An unchanged policy assumption</p>
        <p>{{ pf.model_application.before }}</p>
      </div>
      <span class="pf-assumption-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <div>
        {% for development in pf.developments %}
          <div class="pf-result-use pf-conclusion" data-pf-development="{{ development.id }}">
            <p class="pf-caption">{{ development.label }}</p>
            <h3>{{ development.model_title }}</h3>
            <p>{{ development.model }}</p>
          </div>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
    </div>
    <details class="pf-model-details">
      <summary>Policy rules in the model</summary>
      <p>{{ pf.model_application.after }}</p>
      <dl class="pf-model-parameters">
        {% for t in pf.trajectories %}
          <div>
            <dt>{{ t.id }} · {{ t.short_title }}</dt>
            <dd>{{ pf.model_application.parameters[t.id] }}</dd>
          </div>
        {% endfor %}
      </dl>
    </details>
    <p class="pf-model-feedback">{{ pf.model_application.feedback }}</p>
    <a class="pf-conditions-return" href="#pf-conditions" hidden>Revisit the assumptions ↑</a>
    <details class="pf-note">
      <summary>Evidence for the assessment</summary>
      {% include policy-feasibility/references.liquid ids=pf.overall.evidence %}
    </details>
  </section>
  <div class="pf-sources-footer">
    <h2>Article and supporting material</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="{{ pf.article_url }}">The politics of baselines: A political feasibility framework for energy and climate scenarios</a>. David
      Fornborg, <em>Energy Research &amp; Social Science</em>, 141 (2026), 104981.
    </p>
    <div class="pf-links">
      <a href="{{ '/publications/' | relative_url }}">Publications</a
      ><a href="{{ '/projects/political-feasibility/evidence/' | relative_url }}">Evidence and research notes</a
      ><a href="{{ '/projects/datacenter-tracker/' | relative_url }}">Nordic data centre tracker</a>
    </div>
    <p class="pf-caption">
      The tracker provides deployment context. This project assesses policy assumptions. The article develops the framework through a historical
      transport case.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>About this application</summary>
      <p>
        Knowledge date: 10 October 2026. Decisions: 2027–2030. Scenario horizon: 2035. This documentary assessment uses a targeted selection of
        literature and policy evidence.
      </p>
      <p>
        Reading, translation, coding and presentation were LLM-assisted. Central passages were checked against source text and selected original
        images within the same workflow. There was no independent human double coding or legal expert validation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Illustrations were generated or adapted with AI and depict generic settings. The evidence register distinguishes decisions, proposals, actor
        arguments and analytical judgements. It retains stable source identifiers and passage locators. Web-reader text captures are labelled in the
        source records.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
  <dialog id="pf-source-dialog" class="pf-source-dialog" aria-labelledby="pf-source-title">
    <div class="pf-dialog-top">
      <span id="pf-source-kind" class="pf-caption"></span><button type="button" id="pf-close-source" aria-label="Close source">Close ×</button>
    </div>
    <div id="pf-source-content"></div>
  </dialog>
  <script type="application/json" id="pf-case-data">
    {{ pf | jsonify | replace: '<', '\u003c' }}
  </script>
  <script type="application/json" id="pf-source-data">
    {{ site.data.policy_feasibility_sources | jsonify | replace: '<', '\u003c' }}
  </script>
</div>
<script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/d3@7.9.0/dist/d3.min.js"></script>
<script defer src="{{ '/assets/js/policy-feasibility.js' | relative_url | bust_file_cache }}"></script>
